      \field{abstract}{MUCH OF OUR INFORMATION ABOUT THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN SKIN TONE, RACE/ETHNICITY, AND WEALTH INEQUALITY CENTERS ON THE U.S. CONTEXT. LATIN AMERICA, CHARACTERISED BY THE HIGHEST LEVELS OF INEQUALITY GLOBALLY AND DISTINCTIVE RACIAL/ETHNIC HIERARCHIES, PROVIDES A UNIQUE OPPORTUNITY TO EXTEND OUR UNDERSTANDING OF THESE DYNAMICS. DRAWING ON SOCIOCOGNITIVE THEORIES AND THE PREFERENCE FOR WHITENESS HYPOTHESIS, THIS STUDY USES UNIQUE LAPOP DATA FROM 18 COUNTRIES TO EXAMINE THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN SKIN TONE, RACE/ETHNICITY, AND WEALTH INEQUALITY IN LATIN AMERICA. WE USE THREE MEASURES OF WEALTH, FOCUSING ON ASSETS WHICH YIELD EXTENSIVE INDIVIDUAL ADVANTAGES: HOMES, CARS, AND MOTORCYCLES. RESULTS FROM LOGISTIC REGRESSION MODELS SUGGEST THAT DARKER SKIN SHADES HINDER INDIVIDUALS' ABILITY TO ACQUIRE VEHICLES BUT IS LESS IMPORTANT FOR HOMEOWNERSHIP. OUR RESULTS FOR CAR OWNERSHIP BEST MAP ONTO THE THEORETICAL EXPECTATIONS FOR HOW SKIN TONE MIGHT MATTER FOR ASSET ACQUISITION IN LATIN AMERICA. HOWEVER, WE ALSO FIND NOTABLE VARIATION ACROSS COUNTRIES THAT MERITS FURTHER CROSS-NATIONAL EXPLORATION. WE CONCLUDE BY DISCUSSING FRUITFUL DIRECTIONS FOR FUTURE RESEARCH.}
      \field{journaltitle}{JOURNAL OF ETHNIC AND MIGRATION STUDIES}
      \field{title}{Skin Tone and Asset Inequality in Latin America}
