      \field{abstract}{WE ASSESS THE NONCONTRIBUTORY CASH TRANSFER SYSTEMS IN 17 LATIN AMERICAN AND CARIBBEAN COUNTRIES. WE IDENTIFY 67 (CONDITIONAL CASH TRANSFERS, NON-CONTRIBUTORY PENSIONS AND OTHER TRANSFERS) PROGRAMS. WE ADJUST SURVEY WEIGHTS TO CORRECT FOR THE FACT THAT HOUSEHOLD SURVEY DATA OFTEN UNDERESTIMATES THE NUMBER OF BENEFICIARIES COMPARED TO ADMINISTRATIVE SOURCES. TWO KEY FACTORS LIMIT CASH TRANSFER PROGRAMS' EFFECT ON POVERTY AND INEQUALITY: THE SMALL SIZE OF THEIR TRANSFERS (WHICH REPRESENT SIMILAR TO 33\% OF THE POVERTY GAP) AND THEIR HISTORIC UNDER-COVERAGE OF THE POPULATION LIVING IN POVERTY (ONLY 55\% OF THE POPULATION IN POVERTY BENEFITS FROM THESE PROGRAMS). FORTY-ONE PERCENT OF PEOPLE LIVING IN HOUSEHOLDS THAT RECEIVE AT LEAST ONE NON-CONTRIBUTORY TRANSFER ARE ABOVE THE POVERTY LINE. CHILDREN AND INDIGENOUS PEOPLE ARE UNDERREPRESENTED, RELATIVE TO THEIR POVERTY RATE, IN THE ROSTERS OF BENEFICIARIES. BRAZIL, SURINAME, ARGENTINA, CHILE, COSTA RICA, PANAMA AND URUGUAY CONSISTENTLY EARN THE HIGHEST SCORES ACROSS THE ASSESSMENT CATEGORIES.}
      \field{journaltitle}{OXFORD OPEN ECONOMICS}
      \field{title}{Cash Transfers, Poverty and Inequality in Latin America and the Caribbean}
      \field{volume}{4}
      \field{year}{2025}
